---
import { ChevronDown } from 'lucide-react';
import { REPO_URL } from '../data/release';

// Each answer is checked against the app's code or README, not written from what sounds right.
const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is LiteTerm free?',
    a: "Yes, team vaults included. There is no subscription and no license key, and you don’t need an account: signing in is only for sync and teams, which are optional. The code is on GitHub.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Where are my passwords stored?',
    a: "In your operating system’s keychain: the Windows Credential Manager, the macOS Keychain or the Secret Service on Linux. LiteTerm’s own database holds addresses, user names and settings, never passwords. A private key you store in LiteTerm sits in that database encrypted, with its key in the keychain. With sync on, a copy also goes to your cloud vault, encrypted on your computer first.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it sync between my computers?',
    a: 'If you want it to. Sign in with your email under Settings, Account & sync, and set a master password. Hosts, groups, credentials with their saved passwords and stored keys, snippets, notes, port forwards and known hosts are encrypted on your computer before they are uploaded, so the server keeps only data it cannot read. A computer that already has hosts of its own adds them to the vault next to the synced ones.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I share hosts with my team?',
    a: 'Yes, for free. With sync on, open Settings, Teams and create a team, then invite people by email. They accept in LiteTerm and read you their safety code; you admit them once it matches the one you see. A team’s hosts, groups, credentials, snippets, notes and port forwards are encrypted on its members’ computers with a key only they have. Credentials carry their private keys, so nobody copies key files around. Viewers can connect but not change anything, and when someone leaves, the team key changes and LiteTerm lists the saved passwords they could read.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if I forget my master password?',
    a: 'Use the recovery key LiteTerm showed you when you set it, then choose a new password. Nobody can reset it for you: the password never leaves your computer. If you lose both, you can reset the cloud vault; what is on your computers stays, and is uploaded again under a new password.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is LiteTerm different from Termius?',
    a: 'Both are SSH clients with SFTP, port forwarding, snippets and jump hosts. LiteTerm is free and open source, syncs between your computers and shares team vaults without a subscription, keeps passwords in your OS keychain and adds notes you can run from. Termius has phone and tablet apps and Mosh; syncing needs its Pro plan, and team vaults its Team plan. The comparison table above lists the details.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I bring my hosts from another app?',
    a: 'Yes. Import them from ~/.ssh/config (ProxyJump lines included), from a Termius export file or from a LiteTerm export. Your known_hosts file can come along too.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does it check host keys?',
    a: 'On trust-on-first-use. The first time you connect, LiteTerm shows the fingerprint and asks you to trust it, for every hop of a jump chain. If a saved key later changes, the connection is refused.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it work with 1Password or an SSH agent?',
    a: 'Yes. Choose Agent as the sign-in method. LiteTerm reads keys from the OpenSSH agent (the Windows "OpenSSH Authentication Agent" service, or the SSH_AUTH_SOCK socket on macOS and Linux), which 1Password, Bitwarden and KeePassXC can also serve. Turn on Forward the SSH agent for a host, and ssh or git on that server can sign with the same keys.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do updates work?',
    a: 'LiteTerm checks GitHub Releases, downloads the new version, checks it against the SHA-256 checksum GitHub publishes and restarts into it. On Windows that is the installer; on a Mac the new app replaces the old one, as long as LiteTerm runs from Applications; on Linux the AppImage is replaced in place, and a .deb or .rpm is installed by your package manager after you enter your password.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does macOS say it cannot verify the app?',
    a: 'LiteTerm is not signed or notarized with an Apple Developer ID yet. Open System Settings, Privacy & Security, and choose Open Anyway, or run xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/LiteTerm.app in a terminal.',
  },
];
---

<section class="section band" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow"><b>04</b> · FAQ</p>
      <h2 id="faq-title">Questions before you install.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="list">
      {
        faq.map((item) => (
          <details name="faq">
            <summary>
              <span>{item.q}</span>
              <ChevronDown size={18} strokeWidth={1.75} aria-hidden="true" />
            </summary>
            <p>{item.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <p class="more">
      Something else?{' '}
      <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`}>Open an issue on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .band {
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: var(--bg-2);
  }

  .narrow {
    max-width: 52rem;
  }

  .list {
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
  }

  details {
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
  }

  summary {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    padding-block: var(--space-4);
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    list-style: none;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  summary svg {
    flex: none;
    color: var(--text-3);
    transition: transform 200ms var(--ease-out);
  }

  summary:hover svg {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  details[open] summary svg {
    transform: rotate(180deg);
  }

  details p {
    max-width: 44rem;
    padding-block-end: var(--space-5);
    color: var(--text-2);
  }

  .more {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
    color: var(--text-3);
  }
</style>
